<h1>Asia battery market</h1>
<p>Asia is the centre of battery manufacturing and an increasingly important demand region. Start with China for supply and price signals, then the country pages for local demand and trade.</p>
<h2>What drives the Asian market</h2>
<p>Asia is not one market. China is the supply base: it sets the price of cells, materials and many finished packs, and its export flows determine availability elsewhere. India is a demand story with a localisation policy; Japan and South Korea are technology and premium-supply stories; Southeast Asia is an assembly and two-wheeler story. Reading the region as a single number hides the differences that matter to a buyer.</p>
<h2>How to read the country pages</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Production pages</strong> describe capacity and output — what the country makes, not what it consumes.</li>
<li><strong>Demand pages</strong> describe installation and replacement — what the country buys.</li>
<li><strong>Trade pages</strong> describe exports, imports and HS classification — how the goods move.</li>
<li><strong>Price pages</strong> describe factory-gate and export signals — what a quotation is likely to reflect.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Trade and policy</h2>
<p>Export controls, tariffs and free-trade agreements change the landed cost more than a factory price move. Check the trade page for the route you are using, and confirm the HS code with your broker before comparing quotes from different origins.</p>

